o y  twiddle dot  why is the response and the other  variables are the the predictors  the data frame will have unpacked the  matrix x into two columns named x1 and  x2  and we tell svm that the kernel is  linear because we want to fit a support  vector classifier which is a linear  classifier in this case later on we'll  do a radial kernel  and the tuning parameter is the cost  which we'll set at 10  and scale equals false for this for this  example we'll ask it not to standardize  the variables  and there we've done the fit  and now we can print it and you get a  little summary of of the fit doesn't  tell you too much  except that it does say the number of  support vectors is 6.  so remember in class the support vectors  are the points that are close to the  boundary or on the wrong side of the  boundary  now there is a plot function for the  support vector machines but i would say  it's not a particularly nice plot  function  it's it shows you it  it shows you the decision boundary it  looks a little bit jagged here  it doesn't seem there's much control  over the colors and  as we point out in the book  it breaks with convention and it puts x2  on the horizontal axis and x1 on the  vertical axis  but it's there and it's it's you can you  can get a quick plot  [Music]  if you want but we i'm going to show you  how you can make your plot on your own  and what we're going to do  is the first thing we're going to do is  we're going to make a grid of values or  lattice of values for x1 and x2 that  covers the whole domain  on a fairly fine lattice and then we're  going to produce the classifier we're  going to predict the classified each  point in the lattice  and and then color code  the  plot them and color code the points so  we can actually see the decision  boundary  and so there's some text that tells you  about what we're going to do  and  let's just get to the code you can read  the text  later  so first of all we're going to make a  function called make dot grid and it's  going to take in our data matrix x and  it takes an argument in the number of  points in each direction  and we're going to ask for 75. so we're  going to ask for a 75 by 75  grid  okay and this function make dot grid is  going to do it so make dot grid so  i'll enter it now so  the first thing it does is it uses the  apply function to get the range of each  of the variables in x  okay and then for each of them x1 and x2  it uses the seek function to go from  the the lowest value to the upper value  and and make a grid of length n so it's  very simple  and then now it's got these x1 and x2  now um are each of length 75 uniformly  spaced  values on each of the coordinates and  then there's this wonderful function  called expand.grid which takes those two  and makes a  makes the lattice for you  okay and and then that's what gets  returned so let's apply it x grid gets  make dot grid of x and before we we  actually go into our prediction let me  just go down here and let's look at x  squared  let's look at the first the first few  values so 1  2 to 10.  and so you can see there's the the grid  starting so it's it's it's going through  the first coordinate first holding the  second coordinate fixed  and it'll be 75 times 75 so  so that's 5 625 points are going to be  on this grid okay but it's done that for  you  so back to our code we now predict from  our svm fit at the values on this grid  so the new data we're given it is x grid  and we call the response y grid  so now what i'm going to do is make a  plot and i plot all the points in x grid  and i color them according to what their  prediction was  and there it is  and so now you actually see the decision  boundaries so each of those you see the  fine lattice say each of the points  is  um is one of the points on the lattice  and we've  we've color-coded where they classify so  you can clearly see the decision  boundary  and we'll put our original points on  this plot  and there they are  and so now you can see which ones are  close to the boundary and in fact  on the svm foot  is a component called index and that  actually tells you which are the support  points so we're going to include them in  the plot  by using the points function  and as a slightly different plot in  character and we made it cx equals 2  that's carrick expansion is 2 to make  this these plotting symbols a bit bigger  so that we can clear clearly see them on  the plot and as i as i i promised these  are close to the decision boundary so  these are the points that were actually  instrumental in in determining the  decision boundary  okay so that's using our linear support  vector machine in two dimensions  the function  svm is i do not think that user friendly  and one of the things  you don't easily get from it are the  coefficients  that one would use  to describe the linear function  they code they they can be derived from  the objects on the foot  but  we're going to have to do that ourself  so that's not really described in this  book but there's chapter 12 of elements  of statistical learning which is our  earlier textbook more advanced textbook  and if you look in chapter 12 you'll see  how to do what i'm about to do here and  we've just written some code to do that  so this is to extract the linear  coefficients that describe that linear  boundary  and so i'm going to just go through the  steps here and do that  and  and so  that i extracted beta and beta0  which used the formulas described in  that chapter  so now i'll replot my points on the grid  i'll put my my  my points back in and and the support  vector machines um  the the support points and now i'm going  to use the coefficients  to draw the decision boundary  so this will  it's a simple equation the the the  equations of the form beta 0 plus beta 1  x 1 plus beta 2 x 2 equals 0. and from  that equation you have to figure out a  slope and an intercept for the decision  boundary  so it's a little bit of algebra it's not  too 